This document has undergone three iterations of critical revision and four targeted amendments. Version 1.0 was a philosophical proposal. Version 2.0 became a governing doctrine. Version 3.0 was designed to survive hostile contact with four audiences simultaneously: a UN policy panel, a constitutional court, a military doctrine office, and an adversarial political economist who assumes all actors defect under sufficient pressure. Version 3.1 closes the four remaining structural gaps: coalition fragmentation under China-Russia dynamics, underspecified economic incentives, open-source capability diffusion, and educational timeline misalignment with the governance crisis.

The central insight driving this framework is not alignment. It is **interruptibility** — the structural condition under which correction remains possible even after institutional failure, state defection, or capability overshoot. Every pillar that follows is a derivation of one foundational invariant. Alignment is a technical problem that can be worked on indefinitely. Interruptibility is an architectural requirement that must be built in before it is needed, because it cannot be retrofitted after the fact.

The distinction between constitutional doctrine and adversarial constitutional engineering is precise. A constitutional doctrine states what *should* be true. Adversarial constitutional engineering designs systems that remain functional when actors are self-interested, when states defect, when compute races outpace treaties, and when founders resist succession.

This document is designed to be cited in a policy paper, argued against in a doctoral seminar, and taken seriously in an institutional white paper. That requires it to be not merely coherent but adversarially robust — able to survive the questions it will face from actors who are not acting in good faith, as well as those who are.

