## The Core Diagnosis: Civilization Is Being Built on Single Points of Failure

The era of hero dependency is not merely a cultural pathology — it is a structural design error embedded into the institutions managing civilization's most consequential technologies. Four firms currently control approximately 90% of AI compute. [arXiv](https://arxiv.org/pdf/2512.04119) Nations and corporations are prioritizing speed over security, making safety protocols secondary to economic or military advantage. [dw2](https://dw2blog.com/2025/05/29/governance-of-the-transition-to-agi-time-to-act/) There is no centralized enforcement mechanism for AI governance, and the bodies that exist are often paralyzed by geopolitical divides. [Just Security](https://www.justsecurity.org/121990/governing-ai-agents-globally/)

This is not a temporary condition awaiting better leadership. It is the default trajectory of unconstrained competitive development. No individual actor in the current system has a unilateral incentive to slow down — each faces extinction-level competitive risk from doing so while others continue. The collective outcome is an arms race dynamic that is rational at every individual node and catastrophic at the systemic level.

Changing this requires not better intentions but a different architecture entirely: governance systems designed to remain robust not when actors cooperate, but specifically when they defect. That design challenge is the subject of everything that follows.

