## Pillar I — Constitutional Governance for Frontier AI

AGI is not a product. It is civilization-scale infrastructure, and it must be governed with the same constitutional seriousness as nuclear arsenals, international waterways, and atmospheric commons.

An AGI governance system must be enshrined in constitutional and international treaty structures, with clear limits on power, checks and balances, and mechanisms for human override in critical decisions. [ResearchGate](https://www.researchgate.net/profile/Elio-Quiroga/publication/381259541_Ethical_Foundations_for_a_Superintelligent_Future_The_Global_AGI_Governance_Framework_A_Roadmap_for_Transparent_Equitable_and_Human-Centric_AGI_Rule/links/66997d7c02e9686cd10daa56/Ethical-Foundations-for-a-Superintelligent-Future-The-Global-AGI-Governance-Framework-A-Roadmap-for-Transparent-Equitable-and-Human-Centric-AGI-Rule.pdf) The institutional architecture for this already has a research-backed blueprint. It includes an intergovernmental Advanced AI Governance Organization to set binding norms and standards; a Frontier AI Collaborative operating as an international public-private partnership; and a dedicated AI Safety Project providing researchers with computing resources for technical mitigation of AI risks. [arxiv](https://arxiv.org/pdf/2307.04699)

Five features must be non-negotiable and constitutionally embedded, not subject to revision by ordinary political processes:

**Independent scientific oversight** with no corporate reporting lines and no government capture. Scientists who audit frontier systems must have tenure-equivalent job security, international legal standing, and the right to publish findings regardless of commercial or political consequences.

**Public-interest trustees** with legally enforceable mandates and fiduciary duties to future generations as well as present populations. Trustees must represent constituencies — including non-human ecosystems and future humans — who have no current political voice.

**Mandatory and continuous auditability** of all frontier systems. Not periodic review. Not voluntary disclosure. Continuous, externally verifiable, hardware-level auditability of training runs, capability evaluations, and deployment decisions.

**Distributed Interrupt Authority** requiring multi-party cryptographic consensus for any deployment above defined capability thresholds — detailed in Pillar IV.

**Categorical prohibition on unilateral AGI deployment** by any single actor, state or corporate, without demonstrated compliance with the full governance architecture.

If proactive governance fails, there may be no way to return to a state of reliable human oversight. [dw2](https://dw2blog.com/2025/05/29/governance-of-the-transition-to-agi-time-to-act/) This is not a warning about a distant future. It is a description of what gradual institutional erosion looks like from the inside, in real time, at the current pace of development.

