## Pillar II — Adversarial Governance and Defection Management

This is the pillar most governance proposals omit entirely, and its absence is what makes them philosophically coherent but strategically useless.

Every international agreement in history has faced the same structural problem: rational actors defect under sufficient pressure. States defect under strategic pressure. Corporations defect under market pressure. Founders defect under survival pressure. Open-source communities diffuse capabilities regardless of centralized decisions. Any governance framework that does not account for all four defection dynamics will encounter them in practice without a prepared response.

The Soviet Union secretly developed biological weapons in violation of the Biological Weapons Convention until exposed by defectors — the definitive historical case of what happens when a governance framework over-relies on self-reporting without verification. Robust inspection and monitoring powers have been vital for institutions like the IAEA and OPCW, while the absence of verification in the BWC undermined its effectiveness entirely. [arxiv](https://arxiv.org/pdf/2409.02779) The lesson is unambiguous and must be applied here: verification is not a feature of governance; it is the condition under which governance exists at all.

The framework must answer four specific defection scenarios in advance, not after the fact:

**State defection.** If a major power refuses compliance, the response cannot be moral condemnation — that has no structural effect. It must be: compute export controls enforced through semiconductor supply chain leverage; exclusion from cooperative AI research benefits; graduated trade sanctions calibrated to the severity and duration of violation; and international coordination to deny the defecting state access to compliant compute infrastructure. Non-compliance with governance standards already leads to market exclusion and sanctions in other domains — this dynamic must be formalized into treaty enforcement architecture rather than left to ad hoc political pressure. [Igapa](https://igapa.net/intelligence/global-ai-governance-2026-navigating-compliance-and-geopolitical-currents-5036)

**Laboratory defection.** If one lab secretly trains beyond capability thresholds, detection requires hardware-layer governance, not self-reporting. A Know-Your-Customer scheme for compute providers — requiring mandatory records and reporting for entities that match high-risk profiles — provides verification that operates at the point of hardware provisioning, before training begins and long before deployment. [arxiv](https://arxiv.org/pdf/2310.13625) Whistleblower protections with international legal standing, anonymous reporting channels, and financial protection for disclosures are essential complements. The BWC's failure was the absence of these mechanisms. The IAEA's relative success was their presence.

**Founder bypass.** If a founder circumvents institutional oversight under competitive or survival pressure, enforcement requires legal teeth rather than reputational consequences. The Institutional Continuity Certification in Pillar V addresses this structurally by making institutional independence a condition of operating license — a constraint the founder cannot individually waive.

**Open-source diffusion.** If capability outpaces governance through proliferation of open-weight models, compute-threshold governance has a structural blind spot. The Llama series, DeepSeek, and their successors demonstrate that frontier-adjacent capabilities can be released as open weights, bypass threshold governance entirely, and proliferate at speeds that outrun any regulatory response. The response is not prohibition of open-source release — that is neither achievable nor desirable — but mandatory parameter-level watermarking for releases above a mid-tier compute threshold, verifiable through hardware audits. As open-source large language models become more capable, watermarking techniques embedded into model parameters — rather than applied at inference — provide a viable mechanism to detect misuse that persists through downstream fine-tuning and deployment. [AI Security Portal](https://aisecurity-portal.org/en/literature-database/mark-your-llm-detecting-the-misuse-of-open-source-large-language-models-via-watermarking/) Existing inference-time watermarks are vulnerable to removal by any attacker with model code access, making them inapplicable to the open-source setting — but parameter-embedded watermarks survive this attack vector. [arXiv](https://arxiv.org/html/2410.18861v1) The governance architecture requires: mandatory parameter-level watermarking for open-weight releases above approximately 10²³ FLOPs; a publicly accessible detection registry; and dynamic threshold revision on the same schedule as the compute caps in Pillar III. The watermarking mandate makes diffusion traceable without preventing it — creating post-hoc accountability in a domain where prior restraint is not achievable.

Additionally, governance experts recommend that compute thresholds be revised as frequently as every few months to account for algorithmic improvements that achieve equivalent capabilities at lower compute levels. [arXiv](https://arxiv.org/html/2503.18956v1) A static threshold is a loophole that becomes exploitable within years of being written.

Governance that cannot model its own failure is not governance. It is aspiration.

