## Pillar III — Compute Sovereignty and Threshold Governance

Control of AGI is ultimately control of compute, energy, and semiconductor supply chains. Constitutional commitments govern nothing if the physical infrastructure that determines who can train what remains ungoverned. Mission statements do not constrain capability development. Hardware does.

Training compute — the total number of computational operations used in training — currently represents the most suitable metric to identify AI models warranting regulatory oversight. It correlates with model capabilities and risks, is quantifiable, can be measured early in the AI lifecycle, and can be verified by external actors. [arxiv](https://arxiv.org/pdf/2405.10799) This is not a theoretical proposal. The US Executive Order 14110 already mandated companies to notify the government about ongoing or planned activities above defined compute thresholds. [arxiv](https://arxiv.org/pdf/2405.10799) The EU AI Act contains parallel provisions. The legal and regulatory precedent exists. The task is internationalizing it from domestic regulation to treaty obligation.

The framework requires the following, as treaty-level commitments rather than voluntary industry standards:

**Mandatory reporting** of all frontier training runs above defined thresholds to an international oversight body, with real-time access to training telemetry, not post-hoc self-certification.

**Licensing requirements** for extreme-scale compute clusters — no facility above a defined hardware threshold may operate without international audit rights as a condition of its supply chain access.

**International audit authority** over advanced accelerator supply chains. Semiconductor manufacturers supplying chips capable of frontier training runs must maintain customer records, report high-risk provisioning, and cooperate with international verification as conditions of operating in compliant markets.

**Hard capability thresholds** beyond which multi-party approval is mandatory before training begins — not before deployment, before training. Once a model is trained, governance options narrow dramatically.

**Emergency compute shutdown authority** under distributed cryptographic control, detailed in Pillar IV.

The core treaty provision should be a global compute cap: a binding prohibition on the development of AI systems above an agreed-upon computational resource threshold, accompanied by emergency response infrastructure, whistleblower protections, and an international agency to enforce compliance. [arXiv](https://arxiv.org/html/2311.10748v2) The threshold must be understood as dynamic, not fixed. State parties must agree that initial threshold criteria are imperfect and will require systematic downward revision as algorithms improve and equivalent capabilities become achievable at lower compute levels. [arxiv](https://arxiv.org/pdf/2311.10748)

Without compute governance, every other pillar governs the surface of the problem while the substrate remains ungoverned.

