## Pillar IV — Distributed Interrupt Authority

The phrase "kill switch" must be retired from serious policy discussion. It is rhetorically accessible but technically incoherent, and any expert audience will immediately identify its failure modes: who controls it? What if the controller is captured? What if shutdown is itself weaponized by the actor who controls it? What if no single actor can authorize it when speed is critical?

Replace it with: **Distributed Interrupt Authority** — a multi-party cryptographic authorization architecture modeled on nuclear launch safeguard design, extended to the hardware layer of AI compute.

The technical architecture for this is no longer speculative. Embedding thousands of independent hardware-level security blocks into each AI accelerator, using public key cryptography to check the authenticity of authorization licenses and randomly generated nonces to prevent replay attacks, enables distributed governance models requiring cryptographic consensus from multiple parties before authorizing chip usage — designed to remain robust even against sophisticated physical attacks targeting single points of failure. [arxiv](https://arxiv.org/pdf/2509.07637) This is parameter-level constraint on computation itself, not software-layer governance that can be circumvented by actors with physical access to hardware.

The governance layer above this hardware mechanism must specify the following with treaty-level precision:

**Minimum authorization parties.** No fewer than N independent parties — from geographically and politically diverse jurisdictions — may authorize both interrupt and resumption. The specific value of N must be defined such that no plausible coalition of aligned actors can authorize unilaterally, and no plausible adversarial fragmentation can prevent authorization when genuinely required.

**No single-actor override.** Under no circumstances — including declared national emergency, military necessity, or commercial crisis — may a single state, corporation, or individual authorize or prevent an interrupt decision alone. The architecture is designed specifically to survive the scenario where one actor has every incentive to override it.

**Geographic distribution of authority.** Authorization nodes must be physically located in jurisdictions not under common legal or political authority, such that no single legal action, military operation, or political event can neutralize authorization capacity.

**Automatic audit trails** for all interrupt decisions, with immutable records distributed across multiple jurisdictions and publicly verifiable without revealing operational details that would compromise security.

**Irreversible emergency halt protocols** with separately specified and separately authorized reconstitution procedures. Halting and resuming are not symmetric decisions and must not be governed by symmetric authority.

**Mandatory regular testing** of the interrupt mechanism. An emergency system that has never been tested under realistic conditions is one that will fail at the moment it is most needed. Testing must be conducted on defined schedules, with results reported to the governance body, and failure to pass testing must trigger automatic remediation requirements.

The MAGIC governance framework already proposes maintaining emergency response infrastructure to swiftly halt AI development or withdraw model deployment in the event of an AI-related emergency. [arxiv](https://arxiv.org/pdf/2310.20563) The Distributed Interrupt Authority extends this from organizational policy to hardware architecture and treaty obligation — from aspiration to enforcement.

