## Pillar VI — Constitutional Deliberation on Civilizational Values

The question of what values AI should preserve cannot be answered by declaration. Any declaration of values made by a sufficiently small group, regardless of their credentials or intentions, faces the legitimacy problem immediately: who authorized them to define what civilization is for?

The problem runs deeper than political legitimacy. In many attempts to answer the question of "to what values do we align AI?", the answer has been left dangerously underspecified. [arxiv](https://arxiv.org/pdf/2311.17017) Vague commitments to "human flourishing" or "beneficial AI" do not survive contact with specific edge cases: whose flourishing? Beneficial to whom, on what timescale, at what cost to other interests? These are not rhetorical questions. They are the precise questions that will determine whether AI systems serve humanity or serve the subset of humanity that controlled the values specification process.

By establishing explicit, enforceable red lines, it becomes possible to prevent AI from being used in ways that undermine human dignity or exacerbate inequality. [World Economic Forum](https://www.weforum.org/stories/2024/10/ai-value-alignment-how-we-can-align-artificial-intelligence-with-human-values/) But the legitimacy and durability of those red lines depends entirely on how they were established — whether through imposition or through process.

The solution is a **Constitutional Deliberation Process** — not a fixed registry of values imposed from above, but an internationally governed, cyclically renewable, procedurally legitimate process with the following structural features:

**Citizen assemblies** drawn from demographically, geographically, and culturally representative populations across multiple civilizational traditions — not expert panels, not government representatives, but randomly selected citizens operating under deliberative conditions that allow them to reason carefully rather than respond reflexively.

**Cross-civilizational negotiation** with explicit minority rights protections and veto structures for communities whose core values would be overridden by majority decisions — because a values framework that can be imposed on a minority by majority vote is not a universal framework; it is a majoritarian one.

**Constitutional review cycles** on defined schedules — every seven to ten years as a baseline — with clear amendment protocols that distinguish between core invariants and contextual priorities.

**Explicit tier structure.** Some values are non-negotiable invariants, subject to no revision by any process: human dignity as a juridical floor; freedom of thought and epistemic autonomy; prohibition of coercive control by any actor human, institutional, or artificial; protection of future generations as a binding constraint on present decisions. Everything above this floor is democratically accountable and subject to revision through defined amendment procedures.

Human dignity should serve as the foundational juridical value — a concept that has evolved from Roman jurisprudence to its establishment as a cornerstone of modern constitutional democracies, and which must now be extended explicitly into algorithmic governance. [De Gruyter Brill](https://www.degruyterbrill.com/document/doi/10.1515/ijdlg-2025-0006/html?lang=en) Its status as an invariant is not arbitrary: it is the precondition for the legitimacy of the deliberation process itself. A process that could vote to eliminate human dignity would be using dignity to destroy dignity — a logical contradiction that justifies its protection from revision.

Values must be governed, not declared. And the governance must be as robust as the technology it governs.

