## Pillar VII — Distributed Sovereignty Over Critical AI Infrastructure

The most advanced AI models are hosted on servers in a handful of American data centers, and any country that wishes to compete must rent compute from those corporations. Dependence is absolute. If algorithms remain in private hands, democracy shrinks into a mirage ruled by shareholders. If they fall under authoritarian regimes, humanity becomes a surveilled population. Without a global pact, the future will splinter into digital empires with incompatible values. [HispanTV](https://www.pressenza.com/2025/10/artificial-intelligence-and-digital-sovereignty-in-the-face-of-21st-century-powers/)

The current trajectory is not neutral. It is a path toward one of two outcomes: AI dominance by a small number of corporate actors operating under the legal frameworks of a small number of states, or AI dominance by those states directly. Neither outcome is compatible with the civilizational values established in Pillar VI. Both eliminate the distributed sovereignty condition under which genuine human self-determination at civilizational scale is possible.

The response of this doctrine is deliberate construction of distributed sovereignty architecture — not nationalization, which merely relocates monopoly to state actors, but genuine multi-jurisdictional distribution of critical infrastructure. By embracing distributed, hybrid architectures, societies can capture AI's economic value while preserving their sovereignty. [World Economic Forum](https://www.weforum.org/stories/2026/01/ai-s-distributed-future-a-new-path-to-competitiveness-and-digital-sovereignty/)

At the treaty level, this means multilateral frameworks where critical AI capabilities — the most capable training infrastructure, the foundational models with greatest dual-use potential, the hardware supply chains that enable frontier development — are held in trust across multiple nations, scientific councils, independent civil institutions, and long-term public trusts. Structural prohibitions must prevent any single actor from accumulating the combination of compute capacity, model capability, and governance authority that would constitute decisive positional advantage.

Sovereign AI is not achieved through a single policy decision, cloud contract, or national model announcement. [McKinsey & Company](https://www.mckinsey.com/industries/technology-media-and-telecommunications/our-insights/sovereign-ai-building-ecosystems-for-strategic-resilience-and-impact) It requires sustained institutional investment across four layers, all of which must be distributed: where data and compute physically reside; who manages and secures them operationally; who owns the underlying intellectual property; and which jurisdiction governs access and compliance. Power concentrated in one state across all four layers becomes digital empire. That is not a theoretical future risk — it is the current direction of travel, visible in real time.

