## Pillar VIII — Alignment Incentive Architecture

The framework as described imposes significant costs on actors who comply: compute reporting, licensing overhead, continuity certification, audit obligations, threshold restrictions. Costs alone generate defection pressure. Power responds to incentives as much as to restraints, and a governance architecture that is only morally correct but economically punishing will be abandoned the moment competitive pressure makes defection survivable.

The model for solving this is ITER — the International Thermonuclear Experimental Reactor, the largest scientific collaboration in history. ITER's collaboration architecture allows member states to contribute a portion of total project costs and receive 100% of the scientific results and all generated intellectual property. [ITER](https://www.iter.org/about/iter-members) Despite different contribution levels, each member benefits from the complete intellectual property pool — a structure in which the private return from participation is architecturally guaranteed to exceed the contribution cost. [ScienceDirect](https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S030142152400003X?dgcid=rss_sd_all) That asymmetry is why ITER achieved participation from geopolitical adversaries simultaneously. The incentive structure made cooperation individually rational, not merely collectively optimal.

Applied to AI safety governance, the same architectural logic produces the following quantified incentive structure:

**Quantified rewards for compliant actors:**

Access to a shared international AI safety dataset pool, with compliant labs receiving 20–30% subsidized access relative to their pooling cost contribution — mirroring the ITER model where smaller contributors receive the same scientific output as larger ones.

Shared computational resources for safety research — red-teaming, capability evaluation, alignment verification — reducing individual labs' safety research costs by an estimated 15–25% through collective infrastructure.

International market certification conferring preferential regulatory treatment in compliant jurisdictions: accelerated deployment approval, reduced per-market compliance overhead, and public certification visible to enterprise customers for whom governance credibility is a procurement factor.

Priority access to findings from the AI Safety Project established in Pillar I — safety research outputs that would otherwise require independent investment at full cost.

**Quantified penalties for non-compliance:**

Graduated compute access restrictions beginning at 10% reduction in advanced accelerator import licensing for initial violations, scaling to full hardware embargo for sustained material violation.

Loss of international safety certification with defined market access consequences in compliant-coalition jurisdictions representing the economic weight of the Minimum Viable Coalition (see Pillar XI).

Mandatory public disclosure of the specific nature and duration of violations, with a defined publication timeline, creating measurable reputational and market-valuation consequences that investors and enterprise customers will price.

Civil liability exposure for institutional leadership — not merely organizational penalties — for material violations where leadership had knowledge and failed to act.

The critical design principle, validated by ITER's existence: compliance must be individually rational at every actor's level, not merely globally optimal. A governance architecture where compliance is correct in aggregate but individually irrational will be defected from by every actor facing serious competitive pressure. The incentive structure must make defection more expensive than compliance at the level of the individual actor's calculation, not just the collective welfare analysis.

