## Pillar XI — Transitional Governance and Sequencing

The destination has been designed across the preceding ten pillars. The migration strategy must now be specified with equal precision. Without it, the most accurate critique is: excellent theory, impossible implementation. That critique is partly correct, and must be answered with operational detail, not with additional moral argument.

The transition operates across three sequential phases, each creating the institutional preconditions the next phase requires.

**Phase 1 — Minimum Viable Architecture (0–2 years)**

The goal of Phase 1 is not universal compliance. It is to establish institutional facts of sufficient weight that all subsequent actors must engage with them or explicitly oppose them — making non-participation a visible political act rather than a passive default.

Phase 1 does not require global consensus. It requires a **Minimum Viable Coalition** controlling at least **60% of global frontier compute capacity**. At that threshold: compliant compute clusters can be meaningfully audited; export controls on semiconductor supply chains carry real economic leverage; and the market access benefits of coalition membership create structural incentives for non-members to engage rather than simply defect.

WTO variable geometry allows members to form obligation clubs — negotiated collectively but joined voluntarily — with enforcement confined to club members. Benefits and penalties operate within the club, not against non-members who simply opted out. [Peterson Institute](https://www.piie.com/commentary/speeches-papers/toward-more-effective-wto-role-variable-geometry) Applied here, a 60%-compute coalition can begin enforcement among members immediately without requiring Chinese or Russian participation as a precondition, while creating the asymmetric incentive structure that makes eventual engagement rational for non-members.

The US, EU, UK, Japan, South Korea, Taiwan, and Canada together exceed this threshold. Phase 1 begins with that coalition and treats non-participation by other actors as a defined governance condition to be managed, not a failure of the framework.

Specific Phase 1 measures: mandatory frontier compute reporting by major labs and state programs; voluntary precursor consortium establishing shared audit standards and whistleblower protections; emergency red-line agreements — defined threshold crossings triggering automatic international consultation within 48 hours; chip export coordination among semiconductor-producing coalition members; bilateral military AI crisis communication channels between major powers; and the first cycle of the Constitutional Deliberation Process launched for civilizational values.

A high-level expert panel convened by the UN Council of Presidents of the General Assembly has stated that since many forms of AGI from governments and corporations could emerge before the end of this decade, and since establishing national and international governance systems will take years, the time to act is now. [dw2](https://dw2blog.com/2025/05/29/governance-of-the-transition-to-agi-time-to-act/) Phase 1 is the time-critical phase. Everything else can be built from its institutional foundations.

**Phase 2 — Intermediate Architecture (2–7 years)**

The goal of Phase 2 is to convert voluntary coordination into binding treaty obligations, and to extend the coalition toward broader participation by demonstrating that membership is economically beneficial under the quantified incentive structure of Pillar VIII.

Phase 2 measures: formal treaty negotiations for the global compute cap with dynamic threshold revision schedules; international compute licensing for extreme-scale training clusters; Institutional Continuity Certification as a legal operating requirement for all frontier labs in coalition jurisdictions; shared audit standards with mutual recognition across member jurisdictions; partial Distributed Interrupt Authority deployment beginning with hardware-layer security blocks in compliant facilities; and the Constitutional Deliberation Process completing its first full cycle with outputs codified as treaty annexes.

The Dynamic Watermarking Mandate for open-weight model releases above mid-tier compute thresholds enters force in Phase 2, with the detection registry operational and hardware-audit verification of watermark compliance integrated into existing compute reporting infrastructure.

**Phase 3 — Full Constitutional Architecture (7+ years)**

The goal of Phase 3 is to make the governance regime self-sustaining, self-correcting, and resilient to the political changes, technological shifts, and institutional pressures that will accumulate over decades of operation.

Phase 3 measures: full constitutional AGI governance with treaty-enforced compute caps; distributed sovereignty institutions with operational jurisdiction over critical AI infrastructure; permanent civilizational governance bodies operating under Recursive Accountability mechanisms from Pillar XIII; completion of the first international Failure Metrics assessment cycle with published results; education reform measures integrated into national curricula in participating states through UNESCO framework implementation; and the first institutional sunset review evaluating whether governance bodies established in Phase 1 and 2 have fulfilled their mandates and should be reauthorized, restructured, or dissolved.

The sequencing logic is not arbitrary. Phase 1 creates institutional facts. Phase 2 converts them into law. Phase 3 makes the law self-correcting. Attempting Phase 3 without completing Phase 1 produces the utopian architecture that sophisticated critics correctly dismiss: beautiful in design, absent in effect.

