## Pillar XII — Military Containment and Strategic Stability

Every serious governance framework for civilization-scale technology must confront military realism directly. Frameworks that treat military applications as a domain for civilian ethics to address later are frameworks that will encounter their most consequential failure in the domain they refused to engage.

Nuclear governance succeeded precisely because military realities were placed at the center of its architecture from the beginning — not added as an afterthought after civilian applications were addressed. The Non-Proliferation Treaty, the IAEA safeguards regime, the nuclear arms control treaties between superpowers all began with the recognition that the first and most decisive pressures on any governance framework would come from military actors with strategic incentives to develop and deploy maximum capability regardless of civilian governance commitments.

The same logic applies here. The first decisive AGI pressures will arrive through military channels: autonomous warfare at scales beyond human control, cyber dominance enabling infrastructure attacks with no attribution lag, strategic deterrence asymmetry created by AI-enhanced intelligence capabilities, and command-and-control systems with AI-mediated decision speeds that eliminate meaningful human authorization in practice. These pressures will override civilian governance frameworks if not addressed at the level of military doctrine, treaty obligation, and hardware-layer constraint.

The urgency is not theoretical. We are in what experts call the "pre-proliferation window" — the final period in history before autonomous weapons become as common and unmanageable as small arms. If a treaty framework is not established within this window, the speed of military AI innovation will make any future regulation obsolete before it can be ratified. [Usanas Foundation](https://usanasfoundation.com/regulating-lethal-autonomous-weapons-systems-laws-in-a-fractured-multipolar-order) This window is closing in real time.

The international community has already registered the alarm. In November 2025, the First Committee of the United Nations General Assembly adopted a resolution on autonomous weapons systems with 156 states in favour, expressing concern about "the risk of an emerging arms race, of exacerbating conflicts and humanitarian crises, miscalculations, lowering the threshold for and escalation of conflicts." [Stop Killer Robots](https://www.stopkillerrobots.org/news/156-states-support-unga-resolution/) The political foundation for a military containment doctrine exists. The doctrine itself does not yet.

The framework requires the following as treaty-level military-domain commitments:

**Prohibition of autonomous strategic weapons.** No AI system may be delegated authority to initiate strategic-level military action — kinetic, cyber, nuclear, or biological — without confirmed human authorization at defined command levels. This is the military application of the Foundational Axiom: no autonomous system capable of irreversible civilization-scale harm may become non-interruptible in its action chain.

**AGI escalation treaties.** Modeled on nuclear arms control agreements, these define capability thresholds beyond which bilateral or multilateral notification is mandatory within defined timeframes, consultation periods are required before deployment, and pre-specified emergency de-escalation procedures are automatically available. The specific mechanism proposed is a 48-hour cooling-off period triggered when AI systems reach defined activation thresholds — preventing the escalation of crises by inserting mandatory deliberation time between threshold-crossing events and irreversible military action. [ACM Other conferences](https://dl.acm.org/doi/10.1145/3748825.3748926)

**Military verification channels.** An AI militarization monitoring index using satellite imagery, open-source intelligence, and other data should track the progress of AI weapons development across nations, with an early warning mechanism for AI arms race conditions that automatically reports advances exceeding defined thresholds to international bodies. [ACM Other conferences](https://dl.acm.org/doi/10.1145/3748825.3748926) This extends the compute governance architecture of Pillar III into the military domain.

**Anti-delegation doctrine for catastrophic systems.** No AI system may be granted delegated authority for nuclear launch decisions, biological weapons deployment, or any action whose consequences are irreversible at civilizational scale. Human authorization is not a default that can be overridden for operational convenience — it is a constitutional requirement enforced through hardware-layer constraint on the relevant systems.

**Military-to-military crisis communication architecture.** Bilateral emergency hotlines between major military powers — modeled on Cold War nuclear risk reduction centers — extended to AI-specific crisis communication protocols. These channels must have defined response time requirements, technical staff with AI expertise, and pre-negotiated communication scripts for the most likely crisis scenarios. Coordination between civilian AI governance and military autonomous weapons discussions must identify areas where compatible approaches are transferable — with risks of error, escalation, and misuse manageable only through sustained investment in human judgment and control, and sustained cooperation between technical, legal, and policy communities. [UNODA](https://disarmament.unoda.org/en/updates/key-takeaways-military-ai-peace-security-dialogues-2025)

**Prohibition of irreversible autonomous retaliation systems.** No state may deploy AI systems pre-authorized to conduct retaliatory military action without real-time human confirmation at defined command authority levels. Automated retaliation loops — in which AI systems trigger countermeasures without human authorization in the decision cycle — represent the single most dangerous near-term failure mode in military AI. The time-pressure argument for removing human authorization is precisely the argument that must be structurally refused: if the decision cycle is too fast for human authorization, the answer is to change the deployment architecture, not to remove the human.

A governance framework that addresses AGI in civilian contexts while leaving military applications to voluntary declarations is incomplete in precisely the domain where the first decisive failures are most likely to occur. Military realism is not an obstacle to this doctrine. It is the condition under which the doctrine must function.

